class:: TWeibullRand
summary:: Triggered Weibull-distribution sampler
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Distributions
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/TGammaRand, Classes/TExpRand

description::

Originally developed in reliability engineering, the Weibull distribution models the lifespan of objects (such as the time until a machine part fails). Mathematically, its shape parameter can interpolate between distributions where events cluster tightly or spread out with a heavy tail. Conceptually, this makes it highly useful for simulating natural failure rates, particle lifetimes in granular synthesis, or organic, evolving durations that need to skew heavily toward specific lengths.

Uses the open-uniform inverse transform code::scale*(-log(U)).pow(1/shape)::. Parameters retain the standard Weibull meaning and invalid values return zero.

Like link::Classes/TExpRand::, it produces positive triggered values; unlike TExpRand, its Weibull shape independently controls tail and hazard behavior.

subsection:: Distribution shape

image::weibull-density.png#Weibull densities with scale = 1 and shape = 0.6, 1, and 2, shown in muted teal, blue, and plum. Shape 1 is exponential; values below and above one produce decreasing and increasing hazards respectively.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: shape
Strictly positive Weibull shape.
argument:: scale
Nonnegative Weibull scale.
argument:: trig
Resample on a rising edge.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore the seed and sample a new initial value.
argument:: mul
Output multiplier.
argument:: add
Output offset.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ TWeibullRand.kr(0.7, 1, Impulse.kr(5)).poll }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ SinOsc.ar(TWeibullRand.kr(1.5, 500, Impulse.kr(8)) + 100, 0, 0.08) }.play
)
::

code::
// Granular Particle Lifetimes
(
{
    var trig, dur, sig;
    trig = Dust.kr(20);
    // Models failure rates—perfect for simulating organic, heavy-tailed grain durations
    dur = TWeibullRand.kr(shape: 1.5, scale: 0.05, trig: trig);
    sig = SinOsc.ar(TExpRand.kr(400, 1200, trig)) * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, dur), trig);
    Pan2.ar(sig * 0.1)
}.play;
)
::
